WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.916
Template, code,

00:00:00.916 --> 00:00:07.442
and expression injection risks appear when untrusted input can influence executable logic rather than plain content.

00:00:07.442 --> 00:00:11.113
Server-side template engines, expression evaluators, dynamic imports, plugin systems,

00:00:11.113 --> 00:00:15.192
and JavaScript evaluation features can all create sensitive interpretation points.

00:00:15.192 --> 00:00:18.862
The core question is whether the user controls data,

00:00:18.862 --> 00:00:24.165
or whether the user controls something the runtime treats as code or instructions.

00:00:24.165 --> 00:00:26.204
JavaScript offers powerful dynamic features,

00:00:26.204 --> 00:00:31.099
including eval-style behavior and constructors that can treat strings as executable code.

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:34.770
These features are rarely appropriate for ordinary user input.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.848
Even when they seem convenient for rules, formulas, or customization,

00:00:38.848 --> 00:00:43.335
they can create a path where untrusted text changes program behavior.

00:00:43.335 --> 00:00:46.598
Safer designs use explicit configuration, constrained rule formats,

00:00:46.598 --> 00:00:49.045
or purpose-built parsers with limited capability.

00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:51.085
Template engines also require care.

00:00:51.085 --> 00:00:57.203
Rendering user content with a template is different from allowing users to define the template.

00:00:57.203 --> 00:01:00.874
If users can supply template syntax, expressions, or partials,

00:01:00.874 --> 00:01:06.176
the application must treat that as a high-risk design and apply strong constraints.

00:01:06.176 --> 00:01:10.663
Sandbox claims should be reviewed skeptically because sandboxes often have assumptions,

00:01:10.663 --> 00:01:12.702
escape conditions, or dependency limitations.

00:01:12.702 --> 00:01:18.005
Dependencies matter because libraries may interpret strings in ways that are not obvious.

00:01:18.005 --> 00:01:20.860
A validation library, markdown renderer, expression engine,

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.123
or template helper may have its own rules.

00:01:24.123 --> 00:01:30.649
Reviewing dependency documentation and configuration is part of injection prevention when the dependency processes user-controlled strings.

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:32.688
The safest pattern is separation.

00:01:32.688 --> 00:01:34.728
User content should remain content.

00:01:34.728 --> 00:01:36.767
Application logic should remain application-owned.

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.622
If customization is required, expose a narrow,

00:01:39.622 --> 00:01:43.293
documented set of options rather than a general-purpose language.

00:01:43.293 --> 00:01:47.780
That design choice prevents untrusted users from turning configuration into code.
